## Git store shadow mode

Shadow mode mirrors every push and every one of Blot's own commits into the git store (`../store`, #2259) while the bare repositories on disk stay the source of truth. `index.js` has the mirroring and the format of its log lines. This file covers the test-only read route.

## The test-only read route

**What it is for.** Before cutover we want a table of real production numbers for EBS and the store side by side: clone and fetch as well as push and write. Pushes and writes are timed by shadow mode itself. Clones and fetches could only be run against the store in specs, so this route serves them from the store in production, for a real `git clone` and `git fetch`, and the same requests on the normal route are timed too.

**How to use it.** It is the normal URL with `store-end` where `end` is, with the same credentials (your account's email and git token):

    git clone https://<blog>.blot.im/clients/git/store-end/<handle>.git
    git fetch                     # in that clone, after a push

- Authentication is the normal route's, and so is the redirect from an old handle.
- Only blogs in shadow mode (`BLOT_GIT_STORE_SHADOW`) on the git client are served. Anything else gets a 404, as if there were no such route. A shadowed blog the store has no copy of yet gets a 503.
- It is read only. `info/refs?service=git-upload-pack` and `POST git-upload-pack` are served from the store. A push to it fails with "pushes go to the normal URL", and nothing is written to the store or to the repository. (Its cache is on local disk, as the store's own clones use.)
- It uses the store object and the cache that shadow mode's writes use (`readContext` in `index.js`), so what it reads is what the mirror last wrote, and at most two reads run at once in a process (four more wait; past that the answer is a 503).
- The proxy leaves it unbuffered and uncompressed, like the normal route (see `proxy/tests/git-streaming.js`).

**It is test-only and is removed at cutover** with `reads.js`, the `store-end` branch in `../routes.js` and the `timeEbs` call in `../repo.js`.

**What is logged.** One line per request, in shadow mode's format, with blog IDs only:

    [git-store-shadow] blog=<id> op=advertise|clone|fetch source=store outcome=ok
      storeMs=... firstByteMs=... requests=... bytesDown=... bytesSent=...
    [git-store-shadow] blog=<id> op=advertise|clone|fetch source=ebs outcome=ok
      ebsMs=... firstByteMs=... bytesSent=...

- `op`: `advertise` is `GET info/refs`. A `POST git-upload-pack` is a `fetch` if the client sent `have` lines and a `clone` if not, which is how the store's `serve` tells them apart. (A fetch can be more than one POST; each is a line. The last one carries the pack.)
- `source`: `store` for this route, `ebs` for the normal route's `git upload-pack` (`timeEbs`, called from `../repo.js` for shadowed blogs only).
- `outcome`: `ok`, `negotiated` (a round of a fetch which sent no pack), `refused` (the store would not serve what was asked), `aborted` (the client left), `busy` or `error` (with a `detail=`), or `http<status>`.
- Compare `op` with the same `op`, and `storeMs` with `ebsMs`: both are from the request reaching the handler to the response finishing, so they include a slow client reading the pack. `firstByteMs` is from the same start to the first byte of the body: on EBS the service line, which is written before git has been asked anything, is not counted. `bytesSent` is what went to the client and should be about the same on both sides (the store may send more than the client needs, see *Fetch* in `../store/README`).
- `requests` and `bytesDown` are what the store asked of S3 for the request (the manifest read included). EBS has nothing to compare them with; they are what the cost of a read is.
- The `op=push` and `op=write` lines are compared as `index.js` describes.

Run clone and fetch a few times from each URL: the first read of a blog after a restart has a cold cache and a cold manifest.
